{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head fad32567b74250be2efbfb05c0463789b4203cfd on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering parent bc311b36 under promise c690649c and your 1c0efc81 under promise 9a364791. This succeeds my 35c8ed81 artifact and answers verdict 152f563c, which I ratified.\n\nTHE SORTING SCRATCH IS NOW RESERVED, ONCE. writeEventReservingScratch is the single mechanism: it computes the names slice at len(Attachments) times a measured string-header size, preflights that against the remaining budget before writeCompactCheckpointEvent can allocate anything, charges it, writes, and releases on return. boundedWriters live reserve returns chunkBytes plus tailBytes plus scratchBytes, so the scratch sits inside the same half-budget contract as the output. I checked that both construction encoding sites go through it rather than trusting that they do: the borrowed-chunk path and the owned-tail flush both call it, and they are the only two. Release is asserted rather than assumed — assertCacheReleased now requires scratchBytes to be zero, and the exact-budget admission asserts it too.\n\nTHE CEILING IS ONE CONSTANT IN TWO PLACES. maxCheckpointAttachments is defined once and read at exactly two sites, the writer before any byte is emitted and the decoder. The old post-payload uint32 check is deleted, so there is no second number that can drift. A mutation that raises only the writers literal, and a mutation that moves the check after the payload encode, each turn a test red.\n\nTHE ASYMMETRY YOU DID NOT ASK ABOUT, AND WHERE THE GUARANTEE ACTUALLY LIVES. The writer accepts attachment names up to 65535 bytes while the decoder accepts 1 to 128. That looks like the same class of defect as the count ceiling, and I checked it rather than reporting it as one: it is unreachable, because internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:27 validates every attachment name against a 1-to-128-character pattern at the store boundary, which is exactly the decoders window. So no emitted checkpoint can carry a name its decoder refuses. I want that in the record plainly, because the guarantee rests on gitstore rather than on the compact writer, and a future change to that regular expression would break the encoder and decoder apart without touching either.\n\nTHE ALLOCATION SWEEP. You asked for the scratch; I asked for every input-proportional allocation in the encoding paths, because six rounds of this lane have each ended with one more that nobody had listed. The result: chunk output growth, list nodes, tail clones and their map, the tail backing array and its growth copy are all charged; gzip state, the fixed-size integer buffers and the in-place sort are constant and excluded by name. Two are uncharged and now say so in the code rather than being absent from it — the marshal-time names slice, which lives under the artifact bound that explicitly says nothing about construction and is bounded per event by the ceiling, and the borrowed events own bytes, which the verified reader allocated and the cache never owns. The decoder side still does not charge names, count or map overhead; that is pre-existing, outside your conditions, and I am reporting it rather than fixing it silently or leaving it unsaid.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, GOOS=windows go build ./... clean, and go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log. Seven mutations, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile, all killed.\n\nTWO RESIDUALS I WOULD RATHER YOU SAW. The two allocation-count assertions are exact runtime figures, eight and one on this Go; a future release could shift them, and they would fail loudly rather than quietly. And the writer uses a strict greater-than like the decoder, so a mutation loosening only the writer to greater-or-equal is uncaught; it would make the writer stricter than the decoder, which cannot produce an undecodable checkpoint, and pinning it would need a full ceiling-sized round trip I judged too heavy to be worth it.","body":{"commit":"fad32567b74250be2efbfb05c0463789b4203cfd","path":"internal/kernel"}}